  "_doc": "Reviewed word lists for scripts/build_skill_catalog.py (TASK-082), from 3,218 public postings. everyday_words: one-word labels that are ordinary prose in postings (seen in lowercase in at least 0.5% of them), plus O*NET product names that are dictionary words and read as prose outside tech (chef, zoom, blender). single_word_allow: one-word labels kept although they are frequent, because postings use them for the skill. blocked_labels: labels of any length that matched boilerplate rather than a skill. vendor_prefixes: stripped from O*NET tool names to make an alias. The O*NET single-word aliases made by stripping a vendor or reading a trailing acronym were reviewed the same way: kept when they name only the tool (Kafka, Jira, HTML), refused when they are also ordinary words (Teams, Drive, Outlook, Windows). Bare company names (Google, Microsoft…) are refused: a posting names them as employers and clients, not as skills. generic_vendor_remainders: what is left of an O*NET name once its vendor is stripped, when that is a general concept (SAP Business Intelligence → business intelligence): not made an alias, so the general skill can claim it.",
